\section{Introduction}
Ayoreo (ISO 639-3 ayo) is a Zamucoan language of Paraguay and Bolivia. A bilingual dictionary and grammar were reported in Paraguay in November 2025, aimed at schools.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item News report on the bilingual dictionary for schools: \url{https://www.lanacion.com.py/pais_edicion_impresa/2025/11/13/ayoreos-reciben-diccionario-bilingue-para-las-escuelas/}
\end{itemize}
Ayore.org is reported to host reading primers, but no specific primer URL was seen in the results.
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Ayoreo--Castellano dictionary and grammar notes (news report): \url{https://www.lanacion.com.py/pais/2025/11/12/ayoreos-reciben-diccionario-y-apuntes-de-la-gramatica-bilingue-en-el-chaco-paraguayo/}
\item Ayor\'e--Spanish dictionary (partial, on ayore.org): \url{https://ayore.org/language/language-dictionaries/}
\item Research record: \url{https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/48064/}
\item Endangered Languages entry: \url{https://www.endangeredlanguages.com/node/37589}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Scripture resources: \url{https://www.scriptureearth.org/00eng.php?iso=ayo}
\item Ayore.org (mission-related site): \url{https://ayore.org/bios/}
\end{itemize}
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
Audio and video scripture are listed on the ScriptureEarth page above.
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Ayoreo is not known to be taught widely in schools; the new dictionary and grammar are a promising base, though availability beyond the reported hand-over was not confirmed.
\end{document}
